From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: DIET: Rational Choice

I said,
> > The methodology of many studies in cancer (and other illnesses)
> > etiology as related to diet is as weak as the methodology (and often
> > the same) as people have criticized the FDA for relying on in its
> > rulings.

Perry replied,
> Hey, eat what you like. I'm not waiting for all the studies to come in
> -- its a no lose situation from my point of view.
  (some specific studies removed)
> If you don't
> want to believe the studies, fine. I'll ask this though -- is eating
> fatty meat so important to you that you want to wait until we know for
> sure in another fifty years or so?

I like this debate. This is a really good example of differing rational
choices. Perry, you are essentially accepting a "Pascal's Wager" on diet.
I.e.- the reward is great enough (though not certain),that you rationally
compute the sacrifice as being worth it. I, on the other hand, am not enough
convinced of the certainity of pay off to make what I consider a large
sacrfice. Smoking, on the other hand, was different; I like it, but the payoff 
is more certain and the sacrifice is little enough as to be worth it.

The only reason I belabor this point is that there is a tone in this argument 
that tends to imply that Vegan necessarily follows from a commitment to 
"Extropian" ideals. I know "EC" was perhaps used facetiously, but I still see
this tendency.

> Read Dr. Dean Ornish's work on heart disease. If that alone isn't
> enough for you, well, enjoy the meat.
>
> Perry

Word of honor, I shall. Thanks for the reference. It is always pleasing when 
someone cites something specific.

One thing that is also of interest: some studies have also shown that genetic 
predisposition plays a far greater role than diet in some health problems.
Cholesterol is one major example.  If this is the case, the risk computation
for dietary choices becomes really interesting.

A.

