> No, I 'know more' if I make the (ultimately) skilled player ask
> more than 20 questions on average.  The asker is just performing
> a compressed encoding, based on a "decision tree".  The unskilled
> player is just making a less-efficient encoding.

Someone generating a compressed encoding has a great deal of knowledge -
more than 20 bits worth.

In order to specify a "significant" object in isolation requires only
about 20 bits.

In order to decide that something is an object, to isolate it from 
background and forground, requires a great deal more than 20 bits.

Given that X is an object, it is easy to decide what it is.  The hard part
is the part that seems trivial to us, deciding that X is an object.

