Date: Tue, 8 Jun 93 13:35:28 EDT
From: Andrew S Hall <ashall@magnus.acs.ohio-state.edu>
Subject: DIET: Rational Choice

Lefty says:
> The studies which purport to demonstrate the hazards of eating fatty meat
> or insufficient fiber are done in essentially the same way as the studies
> which indicate that too many vitamins will damage you or that "smart drugs"
> should be banned by the FDA.

Perry says:
>No, they aren't.

>A) They are generally controlled studies.
>B) There ARE NO STUDIES that say that megavitamins or smart drugs
>should be banned. There are just imbecilic bureaucrats.

Lefty stated the case incorrectly, but, as I said earlier, the methodology
is often the same.

The studies that purport to show the ill effects of a certain dietary
substance are often identical to drug testing- mega-dosing etc.
The studies that show a causative link are based on long-term surveys
and other statistical analyses of medical data. They have their own set of
flaws which is why I mentioned rational analysis of value of a certain food
against perceived risk.

Studies that show a benefit are almost always the latter type. Still, if you
are going to accept them, look at all of them. Many contradict one another,
and the case of longer lifespans of the French has yet to be resolved.

My main point is that the evidence of extended lifespan from a vegan diet
is equivocal enough that someone who fully accepts "extropian" ideals
is not rationally compelled to be vegan. I don't want to flame, or
criticize. I see room for honest disagreement. If the evidence reaches
a level that is less equivocal, I will be the first to join Perry in the
produce line!

(BTW- I agree that an extropian veggie cookbook is redundant. I will, however,
be glad to forward recipes to any who ask. I cook a lot of veggie stuff and
believe I have some very good recipes of my own creation.)

A.

